      "text": "### The Dismantling"
    },
    {
      "id": "07-SCIENCE-RELIGION.md#010",
      "path": [
        "07-SCIENCE-RELIGION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:religious-studies:07-science-religion, git-history:religious-studies:07-science-religion]",
        "canonical_path": "religious-studies/07-SCIENCE-RELIGION.md",
        "concepts": "[science, religion]",
        "current_path": "religious-studies/07-SCIENCE-RELIGION.md",
        "id": "maxim:religious-studies:science-religion",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "religious-studies",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[science, religion]",
        "section": "religious-studies",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Science and Religion",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "David Lindberg and Ronald Numbers's \"God and Nature\" (1986) and \"Galileo Goes to\nJail, and Other Myths About Science and Religion\" (2009, Numbers, ed.) document\nthe systematic demolition of specific conflict-thesis claims:"
    },
    {
      "id": "07-SCIENCE-RELIGION.md#011",
      "path": [
        "07-SCIENCE-RELIGION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:religious-studies:07-science-religion, git-history:religious-studies:07-science-religion]",
        "canonical_path": "religious-studies/07-SCIENCE-RELIGION.md",
        "concepts": "[science, religion]",
        "current_path": "religious-studies/07-SCIENCE-RELIGION.md",
        "id": "maxim:religious-studies:science-religion",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "religious-studies",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[science, religion]",
        "section": "religious-studies",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Science and Religion",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\n  Myth: \"Medieval Church suppressed science.\"\n  Fact: Medieval universities (Paris, Oxford, Bologna) were Church institutions.\n        Aristotelian natural philosophy was studied and debated.\n        Thomas Aquinas integrated Aristotle and Christian theology.\n        Roger Bacon (Franciscan friar) developed empirical methodology."
    },
    {
      "id": "07-SCIENCE-RELIGION.md#012",
      "path": [
        "07-SCIENCE-RELIGION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:religious-studies:07-science-religion, git-history:religious-studies:07-science-religion]",
        "canonical_path": "religious-studies/07-SCIENCE-RELIGION.md",
        "concepts": "[science, religion]",
        "current_path": "religious-studies/07-SCIENCE-RELIGION.md",
        "id": "maxim:religious-studies:science-religion",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "religious-studies",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[science, religion]",
        "section": "religious-studies",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Science and Religion",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Myth: \"The Church believed the Earth was flat.\"\n  Fact: Educated medieval Europeans knew the Earth was spherical — from Aristotle,\n        Ptolemy, and direct calculation. The flat-earth myth was invented\n        in the 19th century (Washington Irving's fictional Columbus biography)."
    },
    {
      "id": "07-SCIENCE-RELIGION.md#013",
      "path": [
        "07-SCIENCE-RELIGION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:religious-studies:07-science-religion, git-history:religious-studies:07-science-religion]",
        "canonical_path": "religious-studies/07-SCIENCE-RELIGION.md",
        "concepts": "[science, religion]",
        "current_path": "religious-studies/07-SCIENCE-RELIGION.md",
        "id": "maxim:religious-studies:science-religion",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "religious-studies",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[science, religion]",
        "section": "religious-studies",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Science and Religion",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Myth: \"Galileo was imprisoned for heliocentrism.\"\n  Fact: Galileo affair was primarily political and personal (Finocchiaro).\n        Heliocentrism was not the trigger — Galileo's \"Dialogue\" mocked Pope\n        Urban VIII (his former friend and patron) by putting Urban's words in\n        the mouth of \"Simplicio\" (the simpleton). Urban felt personally insulted\n        and withdrew his protection. The astronomical question was pretext."
    },
    {
      "id": "07-SCIENCE-RELIGION.md#014",
      "path": [
        "07-SCIENCE-RELIGION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:religious-studies:07-science-religion, git-history:religious-studies:07-science-religion]",
        "canonical_path": "religious-studies/07-SCIENCE-RELIGION.md",
        "concepts": "[science, religion]",
        "current_path": "religious-studies/07-SCIENCE-RELIGION.md",
        "id": "maxim:religious-studies:science-religion",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "religious-studies",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[science, religion]",
        "section": "religious-studies",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Science and Religion",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Myth: \"Darwin's theory was immediately rejected by the Church.\"\n  Fact: Initial clerical reception was mixed, not uniformly hostile.\n        Asa Gray (Harvard botanist, leading American Darwinian) was a devout\n        Presbyterian who argued theistic evolution. Frederick Temple (Archbishop\n        of Canterbury) accepted evolution within months of publication.\n        Bishop Samuel Wilberforce's debate with Huxley (1860): Wilberforce's\n        objections were partly scientific, partly theological, and are usually\n        misrepresented. The famous \"your grandfather or grandmother?\" exchange\n        is probably mythologized. Many clergy were not anti-evolution."
    },
    {
      "id": "07-SCIENCE-RELIGION.md#015",
      "path": [
        "07-SCIENCE-RELIGION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:religious-studies:07-science-religion, git-history:religious-studies:07-science-religion]",
        "canonical_path": "religious-studies/07-SCIENCE-RELIGION.md",
        "concepts": "[science, religion]",
        "current_path": "religious-studies/07-SCIENCE-RELIGION.md",
        "id": "maxim:religious-studies:science-religion",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "religious-studies",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[science, religion]",
        "section": "religious-studies",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Science and Religion",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Myth: \"The Inquisition burned scientists at the stake.\"\n  Fact: Giordano Bruno (burned 1600) was executed for theological heresy\n        (denial of the Trinity, eternal worlds, transmigration of souls),\n        not for scientific claims. He is not a \"martyr of science.\"\n```"
    },
    {
      "id": "07-SCIENCE-RELIGION.md#016",
      "path": [
        "07-SCIENCE-RELIGION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:religious-studies:07-science-religion, git-history:religious-studies:07-science-religion]",
        "canonical_path": "religious-studies/07-SCIENCE-RELIGION.md",
        "concepts": "[science, religion]",
        "current_path": "religious-studies/07-SCIENCE-RELIGION.md",
        "id": "maxim:religious-studies:science-religion",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "religious-studies",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[science, religion]",
        "section": "religious-studies",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Science and Religion",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "The actual history: science and religion had collaborative, conflictual, and\nindependent relationships simultaneously in different places and times. The\ninstitutions that produced science (universities, monasteries, patronage networks)\nwere largely religious. Individual scientists were often devout (Newton, Faraday,\nMendel). Conflicts existed but were specific, contextual, and often driven by\nsocial/political factors as much as epistemic ones."
    }
  ],
